              "amount": 2,
              "unit": null,
              "prepNote": "juiced",
              "isOptional": false,
              "groupLabel": null
            },
            {
              "ingredientSlug": "coriander-fresh",
              "amount": 1,
              "unit": "small handful",
              "prepNote": null,
              "isOptional": false,
              "groupLabel": null
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Method"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Toast the raw jasmine rice in a dry frying pan over medium heat for 3 to 4 minutes, stirring constantly, until golden and nutty-smelling. Grind to a coarse powder in a pestle and mortar. Set aside."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Heat a dry frying pan over high heat. Add the pork mince with 2 tablespoons of water. Cook for 5 to 6 minutes, breaking up lumps, until cooked through. Drain any excess liquid."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Remove from the heat. Add the {{fish-sauce}}, lime juice, chilli flakes, shallots, and spring onions. Toss to combine."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Add most of the ground toasted rice. Stir through. The salad should be moist but not wet."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Taste and adjust with more lime or fish sauce. The balance should be sharp, salty, hot, and fresh."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Scatter over the coriander and a small handful of fresh mint leaves. Serve immediately with rice or lettuce leaves for wrapping. Sprinkle remaining toasted rice over the top."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "troubleshooter",
        "attrs": {
          "items": [
            {
              "symptom": "Salad is soggy",
              "cause": "Excess liquid not drained from the pork",
              "fix": "Tilt the pan and spoon off any liquid before seasoning. The pork should look dry before the dressing goes in."
            },
            {
              "symptom": "Toasted rice turned bitter",
              "cause": "Rice was burnt rather than toasted golden",
              "fix": "Keep the heat at medium and stir constantly. Remove from the pan the moment it turns a deep golden colour."
            },
            {
              "symptom": "Dish is too sour",
              "cause": "Too much lime juice for the amount of pork",
              "fix": "Add a small pinch of caster sugar or a few extra drops of fish sauce to bring the balance back."
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Where this dish lives"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Larb comes from the Isan region of northeastern Thailand, though it is now eaten across the whole country. It is considered the national dish of Laos and a key part of Isan cuisine. The toasted ground rice is the ingredient that sets this salad apart from anything similar. Fresh herbs are always involved. Mint is the most common choice, though coriander appears in many versions. The dish is usually served at room temperature with sticky rice on the side."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
}
